  "checks": [
    {
      "Key": "seduce",
      "Title": "Seduce, don\u0027t just observe",
      "Status": "warn",
      "Evidence": "The prose is drenched in horror-first procedural detail \u2014 the Scarab\u0027s carapace \u0027landed to feed,\u0027 the vial drops, the clinical stench \u2014 but the strange-beautiful textures (the schism gravel orbiting a storm drain, the harpy riding thermals, the dead ad-panels marking Lotus territory) are brief asides buried inside long tactical/plot passages rather than moments the reader is invited to linger in and be pulled by.",
      "Fix": "Slow down at least one or two of the genuinely seductive images (the gravel orbit, the Rift\u0027s leaning air, the lattice-breathing corners Levin sees) into fuller sensory beats before pivoting back to plot mechanics, so wonder precedes and outweighs procedural horror rather than flashing past it."
    },
    {
      "Key": "open_grounded",
      "Title": "Open with the world doing its work",
      "Status": "fail",
      "Evidence": "The opening beats (drops in the eye, the manifest, the smell of the rendering plant) are compressed into a handful of lines before the story pivots almost immediately into plot-critical material: a contested account flag, a botched recall, and within a few pages a shooting that kickstarts the entire Lotus plot. There is no unhurried commute or transaction allowed to simply exist before the plot machinery engages.",
      "Fix": "Extend the pre-dawn routine (the drive, the yard smell, the manifest ritual, a mundane pickup or two) for several more beats before introducing the contested-account complication, so the world\u0027s ordinary rhythm is fully established before the plot demands attention."
    },
    {
      "Key": "gloss_in_voice",
      "Title": "Gloss first mentions in-voice, never dump",
      "Status": "fail",
      "Evidence": "Terms like \u0027neuretics,\u0027 \u0027CorpoNations\u0027-adjacent structures (Carrion, Lotus Syndicate, GLMZ), \u0027the Rift,\u0027 \u0027Resonance Zone geometry,\u0027 and \u0027Waxwing Neuromedia civic skin\u0027 are frequently introduced with either zero context (e.g. \u0027The manifest came up on his neuretics\u0027 with no orientation to what neuretics are beyond inference) or with an explicit stop-and-explain clause that reads as authorial aside rather than voice, e.g. \u0027Waxwing Neuromedia \u2014 the ad-overlay grid that painted storefronts with whatever a strip could afford to want\u0027 and \u0027the GLMZ \u2014 the sprawl everyone just called the zone.\u0027",
      "Fix": "Replace the appositive-definition pattern (\u0027X \u2014 the Y that did Z\u0027) with details Tomas would naturally notice and think in the middle of action, letting terms like neuretics, GLMZ, and the Rift accrue meaning through use rather than through inserted glosses."
    },
    {
      "Key": "acronym_after_term",
      "Title": "Never an acronym before its term",
      "Status": "fail",
      "Evidence": "The acronym GLMZ appears repeatedly throughout the story (e.g., \u0027the same shape as the GLMZ,\u0027 \u0027the GLMZ had buyers,\u0027 \u0027in this city, the GLMZ\u0027) with only a vague apposition \u0027the sprawl everyone just called the zone\u0027 at first use, which is a nickname, not the full expansion of the letters G-L-M-Z; no in-voice gloss of what the acronym actually stands for is ever given.",
      "Fix": "At first use, have a character or narration spell out the full term the letters GLMZ actually abbreviate (e.g., \u0027the Greater Lakes Metro Zone, GLMZ, the sprawl everyone just called the zone\u0027) before using the bare acronym again."
    },
    {
      "Key": "human_crack",
      "Title": "Give the POV character one human crack",
      "Status": "warn",
      "Evidence": "Levin\u0027s flash of the photograph\u2014\u0027a man at thirty-one, a few years and a whole life ago, before the trade and before the drops, squinting into a sun somewhere that wasn\u0027t the GLMZ, grinning at whoever held the camera\u0027\u2014and Ekow\u0027s aside about his mother\u0027s name and the coast are genuine human cracks, but Tomas, the primary POV, gets no equivalent unguarded moment beyond functional references to Paz and the therapy bill.",
      "Fix": "Give Tomas one beat of specific sensory memory unrelated to the plot machinery\u2014something of his own life before the trade, the way Levin has the photograph\u2014so the primary POV gets the same human flash as the secondary characters."
    },
    {
      "Key": "land_past_handoff",
      "Title": "Land one beat past the handoff",
      "Status": "warn",
      "Evidence": "The story does land past its plot business \u2014 the kidney return, Levin\u0027s photograph, the queue resuming \u2014 but it overshoots into multiple echoing gestures (the marker owed, Paz\u0027s therapy, the photograph, the three new pickups) rather than one quiet final beat, and the closing line \u0027up into the rest of the shift\u0027 functions more as thematic summary/editorializing about the grind than a single sharp image the reader carries out.",
      "Fix": "Cut the accumulation of callbacks (marker, Paz, queue recap) down to one image \u2014 likely the photograph moment \u2014 and end on that alone, letting the Wagon simply pull away without narrating \u0027the rest of the shift\u0027 as a thesis statement."
    },
    {
